  "status": "draft",
  "title": "Congruent figures",
  "summary": "Recognise congruent figures and use corresponding equal sides and angles.",
  "outcomes": [
    "2.1"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [],
  "estimated_minutes": 55,
  "objectives": [
    "Recognise figures with identical shape and size.",
    "Match corresponding vertices, sides and angles.",
    "Apply SSS, SAS, ASA and RHS congruence criteria."
  ],
  "mastery_policy_key": "g2-default-v1",
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-21-method",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Core method",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Congruent figures can be mapped exactly onto one another by translations, rotations or reflections. Their corresponding sides and angles are equal; orientation may differ."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "\\text{corresponding sides equal},\\quad\\text{corresponding angles equal}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-01-visual",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Read the diagram",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Congruent figures have identical shape and size even after a translation, rotation or reflection. In the diagram, triangle PQR is triangle ABC turned upside down. Matching tick marks show the corresponding sides: one tick on AB and PQ, two on BC and QR, three on CA and RP. Write corresponding vertices in matching order, A↔P, B↔Q and C↔R, before transferring any length or angle."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g2-lesson-01-diagram"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-01-reasoning",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Reasoning method",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For triangles, SSS uses three side pairs, SAS uses two sides and their included angle, ASA uses two angles and the included side, and RHS applies to right triangles with equal hypotenuse and one corresponding side. AAA proves similarity, not congruence; SSA is generally insufficient."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-21-example",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: equal corresponding sides",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In the diagram, triangles ABC and PQR are congruent, with AB = (3x + 1) cm and PQ = 16 cm. Find x."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "AB and PQ both carry one tick, so they are corresponding sides, and corresponding sides of congruent figures are equal."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "3x+1=16"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "3x=15\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x=5"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "Substituting x = 5 gives AB = 3(5) + 1 = 16 cm, which equals PQ."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-01-example-2",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: transfer sides and angles",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Triangles ABC and PQR are congruent, with A↔P, B↔Q and C↔R. AB = 7 cm, angle B = 64° and angle C = 48°. Find PQ and angle P."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "PQ corresponds to AB, so the two sides are equal."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "PQ=AB=7\\text{ cm}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Angle P corresponds to angle A. Find angle A from the angle sum of triangle ABC."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\angle A=180^\\circ-64^\\circ-48^\\circ=68^\\circ"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\angle P=\\angle A=68^\\circ"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The angles of triangle PQR are 68°, 64° and 48°, which total 180°, and every value was taken from the vertex it corresponds to."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-01-pitfalls",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Common errors and checks",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Do not use AAA for congruence. In SAS, the equal angle must lie between the two equal sides. State the vertex correspondence before transferring a side or angle from one triangle to the other."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-21-recall",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A triangle is reflected. Use code 1 if the image must be congruent and code 0 otherwise."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "1",
        "canonical_latex": "1",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A reflection preserves every length and angle, so the image is congruent."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-01-summary",
      "position": 8,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Lesson checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Congruent figures have equal corresponding sides and equal corresponding angles; only their position or orientation changes."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Write the vertex correspondence first, then copy each length or angle from the vertex or side that matches it, and check that the angle sum still works."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
